---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";

const features = [
  ["Voice channels", "Hop in and out whenever you like. Everyone in the space can see who's in a channel before joining."],
  ["Calls in DMs", "Ring a friend directly, with voice or video."],
  ["Camera and screen sharing", "Share your screen at up to 1080p and 60 fps. People on a slow connection automatically get a lower-quality version instead of a frozen picture."],
  ["Noise suppression", "Pick RNNoise or DeepFilterNet 3. Both run on your own device, in the desktop app and in the browser."],
  ["Volume control", "Mute, deafen, a master volume, and a separate volume for each person. Right-click someone in the call to change theirs."],
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Self-Hosted Voice and Video Chat Server | LetsChat"
  description="Run your own voice chat server with LetsChat: voice channels, video calls, 1080p screen sharing and noise suppression, powered by LiveKit on your own hardware."
  heading="Voice and video chat on your own server"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    <p>
      LetsChat has voice channels you can drop into, calls in DMs, and screen sharing. Calls run through LiveKit on
      your own server, and nothing is recorded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="space-y-5" aria-labelledby="features">
    <h2 id="features" class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What you get</h2>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      {features.map(([title, text]) => (
        <article class="card p-6 space-y-2">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">{title}</h3>
          <p class="guide-prose">{text}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-5" aria-labelledby="how">
    <h2 id="how" class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How a call works</h2>
    <div class="guide-prose space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        <a href="https://livekit.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-foreground">LiveKit</a>{" "}
        is an open-source WebRTC media server, and it runs as part of your LetsChat stack. Each person sends their
        audio and video to it once, and LiveKit passes it on to everyone else in the call.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nobody gets into a call just by knowing its address. The chat server first has to record you as present in
        that channel or DM, and only then will it give you a call token. If someone is kicked or leaves, they're
        dropped from the call within a minute.
      </p>
      <p>
        Calls go straight to your server, even if your website traffic goes through a Cloudflare Tunnel. No third
        party relays or stores them. More detail is on the{" "}
        <a href="/architecture/" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-foreground">architecture page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-5" aria-labelledby="server">
    <h2 id="server" class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What your server needs</h2>
    <div class="guide-prose space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        Forward two ports to the machine: <strong>44382/udp</strong> for calls, and <strong>44381/tcp</strong> as
        a fallback for networks that block UDP. If people can join a voice channel but nobody hears anything, these
        ports are almost always the reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        LiveKit forwards streams without re-encoding them, so it doesn't need much CPU. Upload bandwidth runs out
        first, because every stream leaves your server once for each person receiving it. Video and screen shares
        use far more than voice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Voice comes with the rest of LetsChat in one Docker Compose stack, so there's nothing extra to install. The{" "}
        <a href="/self-hosting/" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-foreground">self-hosting guide</a>{" "}
        covers the setup.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-5" aria-labelledby="compare">
    <h2 id="compare" class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">LetsChat, Mumble or TeamSpeak?</h2>
    <div class="guide-prose space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        If all your group needs is voice, Mumble and TeamSpeak are lighter to run and very good at it. LetsChat
        makes sense when you also want text channels, DMs, file sharing and screen sharing in the same app, the way
        Discord does it. There's a page on{" "}
        <a href="/discord-alternative/" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-foreground">how LetsChat compares to Discord</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-5" aria-labelledby="missing">
    <h2 id="missing" class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Not there yet</h2>
    <ul class="guide-prose list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 max-w-3xl">
      <li><strong>No push-to-talk.</strong> Your mic is either open or muted.</li>
      <li><strong>No group calls outside spaces.</strong> They arrive with group DMs, which are up next.</li>
      <li><strong>No sounds when people join or leave.</strong> They're planned.</li>
      <li><strong>No end-to-end encryption for calls yet.</strong> Calls are encrypted on the way to your server, and your server can decrypt them. End-to-end encryption is on the <a href="/roadmap/" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-foreground">roadmap</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>No phone app.</strong> Calls work in the desktop app and in the browser.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3">
    <a href="/self-hosting/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 rounded-xl bg-accent-blue text-white font-semibold text-sm hover:opacity-90 transition-opacity">Set up a server</a>
    <a href="/download/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 rounded-xl border border-border text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground hover:border-border-hover transition-colors">Download the app</a>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
